{
  "titre": "How an air conditioner really works",
  "ligne": "meca",
  "langue_source": "en",
  "script": {
    "titre": "Your AC doesn't make cold",
    "accroche": "Your AC doesn't *make* cold.",
    "description": "Your air conditioner never creates cold: it moves heat from your room to the outside, with one fluid, a compressor and a tiny valve. Watch the full loop in 3D.",
    "hashtags": [
      "#howitworks",
      "#engineering",
      "#airconditioner",
      "#physics",
      "#3danimation"
    ],
    "beats": [
      {
        "voix": "Your air conditioner doesn't make cold. It moves heat from inside your room to the outside."
      },
      {
        "voix": "It all runs on one fluid, the refrigerant, looping through these copper pipes."
      },
      {
        "voix": "Step one: the compressor squeezes the gas. Squeezed gas gets hot, around eighty degrees.",
        "ecran": "80°C"
      },
      {
        "voix": "That hot gas flows up into the outdoor coil."
      },
      {
        "voix": "A fan pushes outside air through the fins and pulls the heat out of the gas."
      },
      {
        "voix": "As it cools, the gas turns back into a liquid, still under high pressure."
      },
      {
        "voix": "Then the trick: a tiny valve. The liquid squeezes through and its pressure collapses."
      },
      {
        "voix": "At low pressure, it boils at just five degrees. And boiling soaks up heat.",
        "ecran": "5°C"
      },
      {
        "voix": "Inside, your room's warm air crosses this ice cold coil and comes out chilled."
      },
      {
        "voix": "The gas, now carrying your room's heat, flows back to the compressor."
      },
      {
        "voix": "And the loop starts over, all day long. That's why the outdoor unit blows hot air: it's your room's heat, dumped outside."
      }
    ]
  },
